  "query_preview": "..Lingering midlevel clouds continue around the \nTucson Metro to the west over central Pima and southeast Pinal \ncounties, and along the New Mexico border in Cochise County into\nthe early afternoon. Water vapor imagery shows drier air \npunching aloft over the White Mountains in Graham and Greenlee \ncounties where convection has begun near and north of Hannagan \nMeadow. Plenty of midlevel moisture has pooled to our south over\nportions of Santa Cruz, Cochise, and southeast Pima counties \nwhere the best instability (1000-1200 J/kg MLCAPE), shear (20-30\nkts effective bulk layer), resides based on the 02/20Z SPC \nMesoscale Analysis. Santa Cruz and Cochise counties continue to \nbe the primary focus for storm formation this afternoon and \nevening. Given the fact that clouds have been around a lot of \nthe day across Pinal, eastern/central Pima counties, my \nconfidence is lower in storms affecting these areas, but cannot \nnecessarily be ruled out heading into the late afternoon early \nevening. The secondary area for storms is across Graham and \nGreenlee counties with a similar shear and instability. \n1200-1400 J/kg of DCAPE resides across all of southeast Arizona \nwhich would support strong downdrafts out of storms that form \nincreasing my confidence in strong gusty outflow winds and the \npotential for localized blowing dust.\n\nMonday is still looking like it will be a more active day for much \nwith the majority of southeast Arizona in a marginal risk for severe \nthunderstorms capable of producing isolated severe wind gusts. The \nhigh pressure will begin to elongate to the west and re-center over \nSouthern California/Southeastern Arizona, creating a broader area of \ndeformation enhancing lift over southeast Arizona. The 12Z HREF \ndepicts a slight chance (10-30%) for strong to severe thunderstorm \nwind gusts leading to a higher confidence for more organized blowing \ndust concerns Monday afternoon and evening.\n\nAn uptick in afternoon storms will continue through midweek with \neasterly flow allowing for continuous moisture and adequate storm \nmotion for the atmosphere to reset each day. The Storm Prediction \nCenter (SPC) continues to place southeast Arizona in a marginal risk \nfor severe thunderstorms capable of producing isolated severe wind \ngusts on Tuesday.\n\nOutside of storms, the Extreme Heat Warning remains in place through \nMonday evening. Temperatures this weekend into early next week will \nbe 4 to 7 degrees above normal. Continue to practice heat safety \nover the coming days. The area begins to 'cool' to 2 to 4 degrees \nabove normal Tuesday onward.",
